#: {{m|ja|所々|所'''々'''|here and there|tr=tokoro'''dokoro'''}}
#: {{m|ja|人々|人'''々'''|people|tr=hito'''bito'''}}

====Usage notes====
In Japanese, this mark is formally called {{m|ja|漢字返し|tr=kanji-gaeshi||kanji repeater}} or {{m|ja|同の字点|tr=dō no jiten||same-character mark}}. More casually, it is called ''noma'' since it looks like a ligature of the katakana {{m|ja|ノマ|tr=noma}}, {{m|ja|繰り返し|tr=kurikaeshi||repeating}}, {{m|ja|同じ|tr=onaji||same|pos=noun}}, or {{m|ja|同じく|tr=onajiku||same|pos=adverb}}.

々 is generally used within a word, and not across lexical boundaries. For example, {{ja-r|[[小学校|小%学%校]]%[[校長|校%長]]|しょう%がっ%こう% こう%ちょう|president of elementary school}} will ''not'' change into *{{ja-r|小%学%校%々%長|しょう%がっ%こう% こう%ちょう|linkto=}}. There are some exceptions:
# in very common words such as {{ja-r|ほげほげ町%'''々'''%会|^ほげほげちょう% '''ちょう'''%かい|Foobar Town '''town''' meeting|linkto=}}
# if reduplication of the same kanji may imply that something that should happen once will happen again. For example, {{ja-r|[[結婚式|結%婚%式]]%[[式場|'''式'''%場]]|けっ%こん%しき% '''しき'''%じょう|wedding ceremony '''ceremony''' site}} may be misconstrued to mean two wedding ceremonies (and therefore a divorce of the marriage in question), so the second kanji may be replaced by 々.

A distinction may sometimes be made between this mark and the vertical iteration mark 〻, but in modern Japanese, 々 is commonly used in both horizontal and vertical writing.

々 is avoided at the beginnings of lines, and instead, the kanji is simply duplicated. However, this is not true for proper nouns containing 々 (e.g. the name 佐々木), which may straddle a line break.

